event MPM: don't exit() from a SIGTERM handler during child startup

make_child() installs just_die() as the SIGTERM handler of a freshly forked
child, and it stays installed until child_main() blocks all signals in
apr_setup_signal_thread(). An ungraceful restart (or stop) SIGTERMs all
children; if one is still starting up, just_die() runs clean_child_exit(),
i.e. apr_pool_destroy() and exit(), from signal context. When the signal
interrupted malloc() (e.g. in ap_thread_main_create()), the heap is left
half-updated and the first free() run by an exit handler aborts with
"free(): corrupted unsorted chunks" or similar.

Terminate with the async-signal-safe _Exit() instead. A child killed this
early has hardly set anything up, so skipping its cleanups is harmless.

Applies to httpd 2.4.68 (Debian trixie) and 2.4.69.

Upstream bug: https://bz.apache.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=70267

--- a/server/mpm/event/event.c
+++ b/server/mpm/event/event.c
@@ -784,7 +784,11 @@
 
 static void just_die(int sig)
 {
-    clean_child_exit(0);
+    /* Only installed while a new child starts up, where SIGTERM may interrupt
+     * e.g. malloc(): clean_child_exit() (pool cleanups, exit()) is not
+     * async-signal-safe, so terminate right away.
+     */
+    _Exit(0);
 }
 
